        {
          "id": "043ef289-c881-387b-b2d6-bc6b4f0a2529",
          "content": {
            "id": "043ef289-c881-387b-b2d6-bc6b4f0a2529",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Nasdaq seeks to extend trading hours to nearly round-the-clock",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: :: File</p><p>The Nasdaq is seeking to roll out near round-the-clock trading of stocks.</p><p>The stock exchange is one of the world\u2019s largest and home to tech heavyweights Nvidia, Apple and Amazon.</p><p>It plans to file paperwork to the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission to expand trading hours from 16 hours a day to 23 hours a day, five days a week.</p><p>Nasdaq\u2019s plans would see its trading week kick off on Sunday at 9 p.m. Eastern and end Friday at 8 p.m.</p><p>:: File</p><p>A trading day would be split into two sessions with\u00a0an hour break at 8 p.m. for maintenance, then start back up at 9 p.m., and would still feature opening and closing bells.</p><p>It follows plans announced both by the New York Stock Exchange and Cboe Global Markets for those exchanges to move to nonstop five-day-a-week trading.</p><p>Investor demand for nonstop trading of U.S. stocks has surged in recent years.</p><p>That\u2019s led regulators to introduce new rules and allow proposals from major exchanges to enable trading beyond traditional market hours.</p><p>Today the U.S. stock market represents almost two-thirds of the market value of listed companies around the globe\u2026</p><p>\u2026and foreign holdings of U.S. equities hit $17 trillion last year, according to Nasdaq data.</p><p>Advocates for moving closer to 24/7 trading argue it will allow investors, especially those outside the U.S., to react more quickly to events that happen outside regular market hours.</p><p>Major Wall Street banks are cautious about the push, pointing to anxiety over lower liquidity, more volatility, and uncertainty over returns on investment.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: :: File</p><p>The Nasdaq is seeking to roll out near round-the-clock trading of stocks.</p><p>The stock exchange is one of the world\u2019s largest and home to tech heavyweights Nvidia, Apple and Amazon.</p><p>It plans to file paperwork to the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission to expand trading hours from 16 hours a day to 23 hours a day, five days a week.</p><p>Nasdaq\u2019s plans would see its trading week kick off on Sunday at 9 p.m. Eastern and end Friday at 8 p.m.</p><p>:: File</p><p>A trading day would be split into two sessions with&nbsp;an hour break at 8 p.m. for maintenance, then start back up at 9 p.m., and would still feature opening and closing bells.</p><p>It follows plans announced both by the New York Stock Exchange and Cboe Global Markets for those exchanges to move to nonstop five-day-a-week trading.</p><p>Investor demand for nonstop trading of U.S. stocks has surged in recent years.</p><p>That\u2019s led regulators to introduce new rules and allow proposals from major exchanges to enable trading beyond traditional market hours.</p><p>Today the U.S. stock market represents almost two-thirds of the market value of listed companies around the globe\u2026</p><p>\u2026and foreign holdings of U.S. equities hit $17 trillion last year, according to Nasdaq data.</p><p>Advocates for moving closer to 24/7 trading argue it will allow investors, especially those outside the U.S., to react more quickly to events that happen outside regular market hours.</p><p>Major Wall Street banks are cautious about the push, pointing to anxiety over lower liquidity, more volatility, and uncertainty over returns on investment.</p></body>",
